# dev.env.example
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local development credentials for rules_engine/ and sampling/.
#
# Copy this file to `dev.env` (at the repo root, same level as this file)
# and fill in real values. `dev.env` is gitignored -- never commit it.
#
# rules_engine/config.py and sampling/config.py each load dev.env
# automatically (via python-dotenv) the first time that package's config
# module is imported, and set these as normal process env vars. From
# there, db/connection_factory.py picks them up exactly as it would in a
# real deployment. The two packages share no code (see README.md's
# "Package separation"), so each has its own _load_env_file() -- both
# read the exact same dev.env, though.
#
# Later this can pivot to AWS Secrets Manager (or similar) without
# touching db/connection_factory.py at all -- only each package's own
# config.py::_load_env_file() would change (both, since it's not a shared
# module), to populate these same env var names from Secrets Manager
# instead of this file.
#
# ONE connection per source_type: teradata, postgres, s3, file. There is
# no named/multi-connection setup -- a rule (gre_rules.sql_dialect) or
# sampling config (gre_sampling_config.source_type) selects its source by
# type, and ConnectionFactory hands back that type's single connection.
# Fill in only the source_type(s) this deployment actually uses.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ── teradata (the metadata store, and typically the primary rule source) ─
TERADATA_HOST=
TERADATA_USER=
TERADATA_PASSWORD=
TERADATA_LOGMECH=LDAP

# ── postgres (AWS RDS / Aurora PostgreSQL-compatible) ────────────────────
# POSTGRES_HOST=
# POSTGRES_PORT=5432
# POSTGRES_DATABASE=
# POSTGRES_USER=
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# POSTGRES_SSLMODE=prefer

# ── s3 (DuckDB-over-S3; falls back to AWS_* / the instance-role credential
#      chain if these are unset) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# S3_REGION=
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_SESSION_TOKEN=
# S3_ENDPOINT=

# ── file (CSV/Excel/TSV/Parquet on local/mounted disk) ───────────────────
# A rule drives its own path entirely from gre_rules (database_name =
# directory, table_name = filename) -- FILE_BASE_PATH is just a fallback
# root for a rule whose database_name is empty/relative.
# FILE_BASE_PATH=/data/inputs/

# ── Shared settings (all optional -- shown with their defaults) ─────────
# GRE_META_CONNECTION=teradata
# GRE_META_DB=CMSUNIV_FILELAND_DEV_T
# GRE_EXCEPTION_CHUNK=500
# GRE_QUERY_MAX_RETRIES=3
# NOTE: GRE_MAX_EXCEPTIONS no longer exists / is no longer read by the
# code -- gre_exceptions detail-row capture is uncapped (every violating
# row is captured every attempt, regardless of count). If this var is
# still set in your actual deployment env from before this change, it is
# now silently ignored -- safe to remove it there too.
# GRE_ENV_FILE=dev.env
# GRE_MAX_FILE_SIZE_MB=500
# GRE_FILE_ENCODING=utf-8

# ── Environment / promotion (all optional) ──────────────────────────────
# GRE_META_DB above already IS the environment-specific value for the
# gre_* metadata store itself -- set it directly to whichever of these
# this deployment is:
#   DEV  -> CMSUNIV_FILELAND_DEV_T   (default)
#   QA   -> CMSUNIV_FILELAND_QA_T
#   INT  -> CMSUNIV_FILELAND_INT_T
#   UAT  -> CMSUNIV_FILELAND_T
#   PROD -> CMSUNIV_FILELAND_T        (same physical database as UAT)
#
# GRE_ENVIRONMENT is a SEPARATE setting: which of DEV/QA/INT/UAT/PROD this
# process is, used ONLY to resolve gre_rules.database_name (the SOURCE
# table a rule reads, as opposed to GRE_META_DB, the metadata store the
# gre_* tables themselves live in) -- see rules_engine/config.py::
# resolve_database_name()'s docstring. Rules are authored once (almost
# always against DEV); promoting the SAME gre_rules rows to another
# environment is then just this env var plus one GRE_DB_MAP_* entry per
# distinct source database those rules reference, never a hand-edit of
# gre_rules itself.
# GRE_ENVIRONMENT=DEV
#
# One GRE_DB_MAP_<AUTHORED_DATABASE_NAME> per source database gre_rules
# references, mapping every environment that database exists in to its
# physical name there. Example for a rule authored with
# database_name='CMSUNIV_FILELAND_DEV_T':
# GRE_DB_MAP_CMSUNIV_FILELAND_DEV_T=DEV=CMSUNIV_FILELAND_DEV_T,QA=CMSUNIV_FILELAND_QA_T,INT=CMSUNIV_FILELAND_INT_T,UAT=CMSUNIV_FILELAND_T,PROD=CMSUNIV_FILELAND_T
#
# GRE_LOG_LEVEL -- see rules_engine/config.py::configure_logging() /
# sampling/config.py::configure_logging(). Detailed (SQL text + row
# counts, never data/params) logging is ON by default; set this to INFO
# or WARNING to quiet it down.
# GRE_LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG
#
# GRE_LOG_DIR -- directory log files are written to (created if it
# doesn't exist). Defaults to "logs" at the repo root.
# GRE_LOG_DIR=logs

# ── Parallel rule execution (opt-in; see rules_engine/README.md) ────────
# Off by default (both = 1) -- rules_engine/runner.py stays single-threaded
# unless GRE_MAX_PARALLEL_RULES is raised above 1, and only ever
# parallelizes sequencing_mode='independent' rule groups. Raising it alone
# does nothing extra to a given source_type until that type's own
# GRE_<TYPE>_MAX_PARALLEL is ALSO raised -- the two caps compose, so a
# source that can't tolerate concurrent load never gets more than one
# session just because the group-wide worker count went up.
# GRE_MAX_PARALLEL_RULES=1
# GRE_TERADATA_MAX_PARALLEL=1
# GRE_POSTGRES_MAX_PARALLEL=1
